From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 21:40:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07200/11362] [outer-loop] REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-19: novelty
 goal-energy fallback for first-contact levels -- real gradient, still no
 plan; plus a self-caught test-fixture-realism bug

Implements REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-18's candidate (a): a self-supervised novelty
energy for first-contact levels (no completion exemplar exists). scores a
candidate grid by distance to the nearest already-observed real grid;
opt-in via CARNOT_ARC_NOVELTY_GOAL_BIAS=1, unset in production.

First live-validation attempt found the branch never fired -- root cause:
_novelty_observed_stack() used index access (t[0]/t[3]) against real
Transition objects, but Transition is a @dataclass, not a namedtuple, so
index access silently raised TypeError, caught by a broad except, dropping
every transition. The 8 unit tests passed anyway because their fixtures
used plain tuples (which support index access) instead of real Transition
objects -- an unrealistic fixture masking a real production bug. Fixed
both the implementation (.grid/.next_grid, the real field names) and the
test fixtures (a _transition() helper building real Transition objects),
then verified the corrected tests actually catch this bug class: reverted
the fix, confirmed test_req_arc_fcp_5699_19_novelty_fires_when_enabled_and_
no_exemplar fails, re-applied the fix, reconfirmed all 32 tests pass.

Live A/B re-run on sp80 budget=250 WITH the fix: goal_energy_source=novelty
confirmed on both arms, min_goal_energy_observed dropped to 0.8875
(baseline) / 0.6765 (sge) -- meaningfully below the binary case's flat
1.0/1.0 (REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-18) -- real gradient now exists. But planned
stayed False and termination_reason stayed max_nodes_reached for both
arms -- gradient alone did not find a plan on this trial, and wall-clock
roughly tripled to quadrupled (429.5s/400.1s vs 63-168s/101-251s) from the
per-candidate numpy distance cost. A genuine partial validation, not a
full one -- documented honestly per Sample-Size Rigor discipline.

